WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.401 --> 00:00:02.063
- [Narrator] When we think of the ocean,

00:00:02.063 --> 00:00:03.905
we think of the beach.

00:00:03.905 --> 00:00:06.272
But I'm speaking to one
of a handful of people

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:09.038
who have been to the deepest
depths of our oceans.

00:00:09.038 --> 00:00:09.896
(phone rings)

00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:11.352
- [Bruce] My name is Bruce Strickrott,

00:00:11.352 --> 00:00:13.874
and I am currently the senior pilot

00:00:13.874 --> 00:00:17.392
and manager of Alvin Operations Group

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:19.749
at the Woods Hole
Oceanographic Institution.

00:00:19.749 --> 00:00:20.582
- [Narrator] Phew.

00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:22.509
He pilots a submarine called Alvin,

00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:24.169
and he works with scientists to explore

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:26.411
deep ocean life and terrain.

00:00:26.411 --> 00:00:28.445
- [Bruce] Yeah, our
average depth on most dives

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:31.647
is between 2,500 and 3,000 meters.

00:00:31.647 --> 00:00:34.960
- [Narrator] That's
almost two vertical miles.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.022
This is what it feels like

00:00:37.022 --> 00:00:40.971
to go 10,000 feet below
the water's surface.

00:00:40.971 --> 00:00:43.091
- [Bruce] We dive, often,
down near the equator,

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:45.049
so it's bright and sunny.

00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:47.912
It's early morning, it's nice.

00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:50.280
You'll dive in the water and
you hit a color of turquoise

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.757
that I have never been
able to see anywhere else.

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:54.701
- [Narrator] But that
blue doesn't last long.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:55.828
- [Bruce] It's easy to lose light

00:00:55.828 --> 00:00:58.886
within five to ten minutes, easy.

00:00:58.886 --> 00:01:00.733
- [Narrator] And you're left suspended

00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:02.816
in seemingly black water.

00:01:03.675 --> 00:01:05.677
- [Bruce] The sub spins the whole way down

00:01:05.677 --> 00:01:08.157
and the whole way up, but
you don't even notice.

00:01:08.157 --> 00:01:09.317
The only way you can tell is,

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:14.254
you can look at the compass
and see it's turning around.

00:01:14.254 --> 00:01:16.677
- [Narrator] Within that
darkness, small flashes emerge.

00:01:16.677 --> 00:01:20.520
These are from animals who
live at these extreme depths.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.124
- [Bruce] You hit lots of bioluminescence.

00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:26.362
The amount that you can
see outside is astounding.

00:01:26.362 --> 00:01:28.649
- [Narrator] And then you hit the bottom.

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:29.722
- [Bruce] When you get to the site

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.280
that you're headed to and
you turn the lights on,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.440
there's a huge wow factor there.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.638
- [Narrator] In particular,
areas with geothermal vents

00:01:37.638 --> 00:01:41.605
or these underwater
geysers, massive structures

00:01:41.605 --> 00:01:45.557
that rise thousands of feet
up towards the surface.

00:01:45.557 --> 00:01:46.630
- [Bruce] The view out the front

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.234
is astounding because
there's nothing like it

00:01:48.234 --> 00:01:50.357
anywhere else on the planet, nothing.

00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:52.452
Our perspective is
altered by our experience

00:01:52.452 --> 00:01:54.197
because you can't go and
look at the ocean anymore

00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:57.168
and think of it just as this
endless, flat surface of water.

00:01:57.168 --> 00:01:59.920
You have an understanding
of what's underneath it.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.753
(inspiring music)

